//! member: true
//!
//! std.ops — the operator traits: the protocols `+ - * / %` and unary
//! `-` dispatch through when their operand is a type parameter or a user
//! type. Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **This module is what `[type.trait.op]` asks std for, and the shapes
//! are the clause's, not this module's opinion.** Since s155 (wolf-lang
//! #5, spec/10 §11) an operator whose left operand is a type parameter
//! or a user type is a call to a trait method chosen BY NAME: `a + b` is
//! `Add.add(a, b)`, `-a` is `Neg.neg(a)`. The compiler refuses any other
//! method shape with E0514, a missing impl with E0502 and no trait in
//! scope with E0301, so the six declarations below are transcriptions
//! and a rename here is a break for every caller.
//!
//! **On primitives the impls ARE the builtin operations** — the clause
//! says so in as many words, so `[T: Add]` instantiated at `int` is the
//! machine add and nothing is interposed. Every body below is one
//! operator expression for that reason; a body that did arithmetic of
//! its own would be a different function wearing the operator's name.
//!
//! **`Eq` and `Ord` are NOT here.** They are `std.cmp`'s and they stay
//! there: `Ordering` is one enum in std (API-CONVENTIONS §6) and the
//! compiler reads `Ord.cmp`'s answer by VARIANT NAME, so a second
//! `Ordering` would be a second answer to a settled question. A bound
//! that wants both writes `[T: ops.Add + cmp.Ord]`; the composed alias
//! `Num` is the next paragraph's subject.
//!
//! **`trait Num` SHIPS HERE, AND IT IS THE LAST ITEM OF s155's SURFACE
//! THIS MODULE OWED.** `[type.trait.op.alias]` spells it `trait Num =
//! Add + Sub + Mul + Div + Rem + Eq + Ord` and wolf-std#26 asked for it
//! by name; sc44 withheld it and wrote its own trigger — "the
//! wolf-interp release whose conformance pin is at or past wolf-lang
//! `c9237c1` (v0.2.11)". is46 is that release: lupin 0.1.34 pins
//! `c9237c1` and parses the alias form (wolf-interp#92). Measured
//! before a line was written, not inherited from the release note.
//! `Eq` and `Ord` are qualified because wolf binds whole modules and
//! has no item imports, so the composed bound a caller writes is
//! `[T: ops.Num]` and nothing else.
//!
//! **The second trigger is met too, so `Num` is satisfiable at `f64` on
//! the day it lands rather than a release later.** `impl Rem for f64`
//! was the half sc44 withheld with `Num`: its body is `self % other` by
//! the clause, and at 0.2.11 BOTH compiler tiers refused that expression
//! (`no frem op` natively, `unruled: fmod vs remainder` checked), which
//! cost every importer a lane rather than costing this one impl. s156's
//! `[type.float.rem]` (wolf-lang#327) rules it and lowers it —
//! truncated-division remainder, sign of the dividend, `x % 0.0` is NaN,
//! nothing traps — and the impl is below, measured running on all three
//! lanes.
//!
//! INTERIM SPELLINGS (each filed, flipped by the pin bump that fixes it):
//! - **`Num`'s own examples are PROSE, and the six traits' are not.**
//!   The two reasons sc44 wrote here are both gone: a qualified trait in
//!   a BOUND counts as a use of its import on lupin now
//!   (wolf-interp#97), and trait dispatch through an imported module's
//!   trait executes there (wolf-interp#96) — so every example below is a
//!   fenced block that runs on the reference machine, which is what §4
//!   wants and what this module could not give until this release. The
//!   ONE exception is the alias: an alias reached through an import
//!   expands no bound under lupin 0.1.34, so `fn f[T: ops.Num]` is
//!   `E0501: the bounds on `T` say nothing about `+`` at resolve while
//!   `fn f[T: ops.Add]` through the same import runs, and both compiler
//!   rungs run the alias at `int` and at `f64`. Filed as
//!   wolf-interp#102. Doc-truth wants the reference machine at
//!   `exit(0)` (§4), so `Num`'s example stays prose until it closes —
//!   and `tests/ops/num_alias_tier.lu` carries the measurement as the
//!   ledger's first `mirror-lag(E0501)` row, which is the word sc46
//!   added for exactly this shape (wolf-std#27, F-0121).
//! - `impl Div for int` is integer division, truncating toward zero, and
//!   it traps `div-zero` on a zero divisor exactly as the operator does —
//!   the impl IS the operator, traps included, which is what
//!   `tests/ops/div_zero_trap.lu` is for.

use std.cmp

/// The composed bound, `[type.trait.op.alias]`'s own example and
/// the one the book's chapter 5 writes: `fn total[T: ops.Num](xs:
/// List[T], zero: T) -> T`. An alias is a memberless trait carrying a
/// bound — there is no supertrait clause this edition, so composition
/// is spelled here and `Ord requires Eq` stays documentary in std.cmp.
///
/// `Eq` and `Ord` are `cmp.`-qualified and stay std.cmp's: `Ordering` is
/// one enum in std (API-CONVENTIONS §6) and the compiler reads
/// `Ord.cmp`'s answer by VARIANT NAME, so a second `Ordering` here would
/// be a second answer to a settled question.
///
/// Prose, not a fenced example, and the reason is filed: an alias
/// reached through an IMPORT expands no bound under lupin 0.1.34
/// (wolf-interp#102), so a fence here could not reach `exit(0)` on the
/// reference machine and §4 sends it to prose. Under `fn total[T:
/// ops.Num](xs: List[T], zero: T)`, `total` compiles and runs at `int`
/// and at `f64` on both compiler rungs; `tests/ops/num_alias_tier.lu`
/// is the running witness and the ledger row that reds the day
/// wolf-interp#102 closes.
pub trait Num = Add + Sub + Mul + Div + Rem + cmp.Eq + cmp.Ord

/// Addition, `a + b`. The compiler dispatches `+` here when the left
/// operand is a type parameter bounded by `Add` or a user type with an
/// `Add` impl in scope ([type.trait.op]); both operands are the same
/// type, which the clause calls homogeneity and E0514 enforces.
///
/// Under `fn total[T: ops.Add](xs: List[T], zero: T)`, `acc = acc + x`
/// is `Add.add(acc, x)`, and at `int` that is the machine add — which
/// is what the fence asserts, since a doc example declares no functions
/// of its own.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let a: int = 2
/// let b: int = 3
/// ops.Add.add(a, b) == 5
/// ```
pub trait Add {
    fn add(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Subtraction, `a - b`. Binary only: unary minus is `Neg`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let a: int = 9
/// let b: int = 4
/// ops.Sub.sub(a, b) == 5
/// ```
pub trait Sub {
    fn sub(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Multiplication, `a * b`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let a: int = 6
/// let b: int = 7
/// ops.Mul.mul(a, b) == 42
/// ```
pub trait Mul {
    fn mul(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Division, `a / b`. The impl inherits the operator's traps: at `int`
/// a zero divisor traps `divide_by_zero`, at `f64` it is IEEE and traps
/// nothing.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let a: int = 7
/// let b: int = 2
/// ops.Div.div(a, b) == 3
/// let x: f64 = 7.5
/// let y: f64 = 2.0
/// ops.Div.div(x, y) == 3.75
/// ```
pub trait Div {
    fn div(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Remainder, `a % b`. At `int` the sign follows the dividend
/// (truncated remainder) and a zero divisor traps `divide_by_zero`; at
/// `f64` it is `fmod` by `[type.float.rem]` — the remainder of the
/// truncated division, the sign of the dividend again, and nothing
/// traps. The `f64` impl landed at sc46 with the clause that lowered
/// the operator (wolf-lang#327).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let a: int = 17
/// let b: int = 5
/// ops.Rem.rem(a, b) == 2
/// let x: f64 = 7.5
/// let y: f64 = 2.0
/// ops.Rem.rem(x, y) == 1.5
/// ```
pub trait Rem {
    fn rem(self, other: Self) -> Self
}

/// Arithmetic negation, unary `-a`. One argument, unlike the five
/// binary traits, and the clause reads the shape: a `neg` taking two
/// parameters is E0514.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let a: int = 5
/// ops.Neg.neg(a) == 0 - 5
/// ```
pub trait Neg {
    fn neg(self) -> Self
}

impl Add for int {
    fn add(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self + other
    }
}

impl Sub for int {
    fn sub(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self - other
    }
}

impl Mul for int {
    fn mul(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self * other
    }
}

impl Div for int {
    fn div(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self / other
    }
}

impl Rem for int {
    fn rem(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self % other
    }
}

impl Neg for int {
    fn neg(self) -> Self {
        -self
    }
}

impl Add for f64 {
    fn add(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self + other
    }
}

impl Sub for f64 {
    fn sub(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self - other
    }
}

impl Mul for f64 {
    fn mul(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self * other
    }
}

impl Div for f64 {
    fn div(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self / other
    }
}

// sc46: the impl sc44 withheld, landed on the trigger it named.
// wolf-lang#327 went the lowering way — `[type.float.rem]`, `fmod`,
// `Opcode::Frem` through the whole set — so `self % other` is served on
// both compiler tiers and on the reference machine, and the line that
// said an importer would drop from `exit(0)` to `unsupported` retires
// with it. Measured on all three lanes before this impl was written.
impl Rem for f64 {
    fn rem(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self % other
    }
}

impl Neg for f64 {
    fn neg(self) -> Self {
        -self
    }
}

/// Concatenation, `a + b` ([type.str.concat]). `str`'s one operator
/// impl: there is no `Sub`, `Mul`, `Div` or `Rem` for `str`, because a
/// string has no such operation and an impl that invented one would be
/// a lie the operator table tells for it.
impl Add for str {
    fn add(self, other: Self) -> Self {
        self + other
    }
}

// BLOCKED — the bitwise family (`BitAnd`, `BitOr`, `BitXor`, `Shl`,
// `Shr`, `Not`) and `Index`. [type.trait.op]'s table this edition is the
// six arithmetic operators plus `Eq`/`Ord`'s comparisons; nothing
// upstream dispatches `&`, `|`, `^`, `<<`, `>>` or `xs[i]` through a
// trait, so a trait named for one would be a declaration no compiler
// reads. They land with the clause that adds them to the table.
